imagine standing at the origin of an  infinite number line  you begin flipping a fair coin that  tells you where you should move  if you get tails you take one step to  the left and if you get heads  one step to the right this is a random  walk in one dimension and more  specifically this is a symmetric  random walk where we have equal  probability of moving in either  direction  we can generalize this type of walk to  any number of dimensions  randomly selecting a unit step in any  axis direction  so in two dimensions we can take a step  along the x or y axis  and in three dimensions along the x y or  z axis  in the early 1900's hungarian  mathematician  george poglia was interested in the  following question  once we begin our random walk are we  guaranteed to eventually return  to where we started we might think that  if we walk forever we'll surely come  back at some point  however long it takes but when it comes  to infinity  our intuitions can mislead us it turns  out while this is correct in the  one and two dimensional cases in three  dimensions this is suddenly no  longer true and in fact it's more likely  that we never return  our probability of returning drops from  a clean one  to .34 as it's often summarized  a drunk man will find his way home but a  drunk bird  may get lost forever now you're probably  asking why  why this abrupt change in behavior as we  jump from two dimensions  to three sure as we move to higher  dimensional space we have  more choices for our steps and it makes  sense that it would probably take  longer to return but to never return  that's strange in this video  we'll try to gain some intuition about  this awesome result  known as polia's recurrence theorem  let's first formalize the question a bit  let s  n be our location at step n then  sn is the sum of each of our individual  steps x  i as i ranges from 1 to n  each step x i is drawn uniformly among  the axis aligned unit steps  so for a given dimension d there are two  times d  possible steps and each has an equal  chance of selection  now familiarity with markov chains won't  be required to understand anything in  this video  but if you have seen them before you  know that we can view the walk as a  markov chain  with an infinite set of possible states  every d  dimensional lattice point with  transition probabilities 1 over 2d  for jumping to each neighboring state  and 0 for jumping to any farther state  we're concerned with this probability  the probability  of returning to the origin at least once  if this probability equals 1 the origin  will be called a recurrent state  but if there's some non-zero chance of  never returning  it's transient suppose the origin is  known to be recurrent  then when we eventually do return we  will basically begin a brand new walk  again with the guarantee of returning  naturally we might expect to visit the  origin  infinitely many times over an infinite  time frame  for these types of systems this  condition turns out to be both necessary  and sufficient  we have recurrence if and only if the  expected number of returns  is infinite let's take a look at some  walks in one dimension  and see if we notice anything about  their behavior  we'll set a hundred walkers to each take  10 steps and record their final  positions  one thing we see of course is that since  we had an even number of time steps  we have zero walkers on the odd  coordinates but there also seems to be a  higher concentration of walkers near the  origin  let's try a thousand steps with ten  thousand walkers  our walkers are more spread out now but  we still see this bell-shaped curve  which may remind you of a normal or  gaussian distribution  the central limit theorem tells us that  the distribution of the sum  of independent and identically  distributed random variables  with finite mean and variance ultimately  converges to a normal distribution  well we definitely meet those conditions  here the mean of our two step choices  is zero and our squared step length is  always one  so if the sum of our individual steps is  approaching a normal distribution  what is its width how far do we expect  to get  let's compute the average squared  distance from the origin at step n  this is also equivalent to the variance  of sn because  sn is a centered random variable the  expected position  no matter how many steps we take is  still zero the origin  the squared distance will just be the  square of the sum of our steps  linearity of expectation allows us to  bring the expectation operator  inside these two summations  we already know that the square of each  step length is one  but what is the expectation of x i times  xj  that is the product of two independent  steps  well half the time the step directions  will be the same  and half different likewise the product  is  negative one or one with equal frequency  so the expectation of this product is  zero and overall  we end up with a sum of n ones  so our average square distance is just n  and our root mean squared distance the  standard deviation of sn  is the square root of n you're likely  familiar with the  6895 99.7 rule  where we expect 68 95 and 99.7  of the values of a normal distribution  to lie within one  two and three standard deviations of the  mean respectively  so let's first think heuristically about  our probability of being at the origin  at a given step since it's only possible  to return  in an even number of steps we'll ask  what's the probability of returning on  step  2n well if our distribution is  approximately normal  we expect to end up within one standard  deviation of the mean  the majority of the time for two n steps  our standard deviation is radical two n  and there are about  two times radical two n positions within  this distance from the origin  with half of them being even if we treat  these options uniformly  we might guess that our probability of  being at the origin is on the order of  1 over radical 2n now this constant is  probably a bit off because  we only expect to be within this  interval 68  of the time and in truth we should have  a bit more probability mass  for positions closer to the origin but  it turns out this is a pretty good  approximation  to be a little more accurate let's  determine how many possible trajectories  there are  of length two n for each step we have  two choices  negative and positive so that's two  raised to the two nth power  distinct trajectories how many of these  have us back at the origin  at step 2 n well such a trajectory would  be required to have exactly as many  negative steps  as positive steps we can simply focus on  which steps will be designated as  positive while the rest will be negative  so how many ways are there to select n  indices out of 2n  total indices that would be the binomial  coefficient  2n choose n so our probability of being  back at the origin  is 2n choose n over 2 to the 2n  the number of suitable trajectories  divided by the total number of  trajectories  great but now we have several factorials  which are difficult to evaluate  to help we can bring in sterling's  formula which is known to be  asymptotically equivalent to the  factorial  the derivation for sterling's formula is  a bit involved and deserves its own  separate video  at the moment let's just take it off the  shelf and plug it in  after simplifying we end up with this  one over radical pi  n so we were pretty close the  probability decreases proportionally  to 1 over radical n with this in hand  let's evaluate the expectation of the  number of visits  in one dimension the summand here is an  indicator variable where it equals 1 if  s2n  is the origin and 0 otherwise  we end up with a p series where p our  power  is one half if we remember from calculus  a p  series with p less than or equal to one  ends up diverging because the summand  decreases too slowly  regardless of any constant factor  multiplying it  the expected number of returns to the 