nsions we have  probability one  of visiting every point with infinite  expected visits for each  recurrence at the origin implies  recurrence everywhere  now algebraically this all might be  convincing  but still why this abrupt change from  two to three dimensions  what ultimately clicked for me was  understanding that higher dimensional  walks  only behave like two-dimensional objects  expanding over time  alright what does that mean  to understand let's revisit the question  of average distance traveled  before we proved that the root mean  squared distance of a one-dimensional  walker's position  at step n is root n  it turns out that this square root  relationship holds in any number of  dimensions  let s n i be the i-coordinate of the  walker at step n  the expected squared distance along the  i-axis  will be equal to the number of steps  taken along that axis  so the expected squared total distance  is n  regardless of the dimensionality of the  walk  what if we ask a related question when  we first reach a distance r  how many steps do we expect to have  taken  similarly this ends up being r squared  steps  this is really the key suppose that  optimistically  when a three-dimensional walk reaches  distance r  all of its r-squared expected steps  visited distinct points  with zero repeats but the total number  of points within this distance will grow  like r  cubed as time goes on the walk will  cover an ever decreasing fraction of  these points  proportional to r squared over r cubed  or one over r and won't be able to catch  up  so the rate at which three-dimensional  walks move away from the origin  is too great relative to this coverage  rate  in the limit even though a  three-dimensional walk will visit  infinitely many points  it will only visit zero percent of the  entire state space  the origin can't be recurrent because we  know  as with the one and two dimensional  cases recurrence would imply  visiting every point and while sure it's  possible for it to return to the origin  a few times by chance  eventually it will get lost forever the  walk is like  an expanding two-dimensional object  because they exhibit similar ratios of  distance reached versus expected  coverage  if a circle lying on the z equals zero  plane begins expanding  when it reaches distance r it will only  have encompassed roughly  pi r-squared points an ever-decreasing  fraction of the bounding sphere  of radius r in two dimensions  the expected r squared steps ensure that  the walk never falls too far behind  the coverage rate ends up being  sufficient to eventually visit all the  points  infinitely many times with probability  1.  for some more on random walks and  various proofs of polio's recurrence  theorem  check out the links in the description  thanks for watching

